lem in the united states um  is that because all of us have gone  through education uh most of us fancy  ourselves as experts in education uh and  i i tell folks if you don't believe that  next time you're checking out at the  grocery store ask the either the person  in front of you or behind you  if there are problems in education and  how to fix them and they definitely have  answers  for both of those questions  so  for the logic to hold true  at least in my view it would have to  hold true in another context you know  this past year my daughter  who was riding her bike and and fell off  uh and skinned her knee um yeah a minor  small abrasion  and  this is not a function of of my income  um but i felt  um competent enough uh to know uh to not  use hydrogen peroxide to use rubbing  alcohol and clean it and apply bandage  uh and and it generally was fine and she  healed just well um but comparatively  if my daughter had fallen off of her  bike um and and broken her leg uh say  broken her femur compound fracture the  bonus through the skin  um would i in the same situation given  the same background training the same  income would i feel competent and  qualified enough to tend to her injuries  and the answer i think for any of you  viewers would be obviously not uh and  and we also know that if i uh did  pretend or fancy myself as qualified to  do that i may come under trouble uh for  neglect uh for my child if i don't seek  medical attention for her in that  particular instance  so  is somebody who makes enough money  qualified um  i don't know that that's the the i don't  know that a good answer to that question  should follow in the sense of  uh if it's not true across any other  area of child development it's probably  and likely not true here in education  just having money  does not make one an expert in how to  deliver  content how to engage in pedagogy or  understand  child development in such a way  that lessons are age-appropriate and not  just in the content sense but  even how the the childhood  child brain understands hypothetical  questions or it doesn't  these are things that professional  educators learn in their training and  through experience  and at least in my view makes them  exceedingly more qualified  to deliver education to children  probably doesn't teach things like crt  either  you're at home  i mean if you make more money it doesn't  mean that you know about that either  no yeah absolutely um you know i myself  i have a doctorate in education policy  uh i never once had a class in crt all  the way through graduate school uh and  so i i i mean i i have a general  understanding what it is but i i am no  beans an expert in crt  um  where did you get your education sure i  did my bachelor's degree at valdosta  state university  and that was my initial training  to be a  middle to high school history teacher i  did my master's at georgia state  university and my phd at the university  of illinois in urbana-champaign  and was what year was that if you don't  mind me asking of course i graduated  with my phd in  2016  my master's in 2012 and my bachelor's in  2007.  okay are you saying so you said what you  do now is basically educate people on  how to educate people  that's right so my students are  most of them are sophomores and as an  undergrad student  who are taking a sequence of courses  prior to applying for entry into  the college of education of course that  would be a subset of the university  itself  um and so i teach courses on critical  and contemporary issues in education  educational psychology  and things of that nature  okay and you said you haven't come  across crt or that's not being taught  very much from what you've seen  yeah throughout my entire coursework  i did not take a course on crt and and  that's certainly not anything that we  teach  current  training teachers as best as i  understand the the formal groundings of  crt it's a  um something taught in in law schools  that stem