its of  public schools  um the downfalls that that need to be  attended to and fixed um but  collectively fix that together as  opposed to um  pulling away from that that possibility  because public schools do represent  the  the best opportunity that we have  to provide all children with an equal  opportunity we know currently that they  do not we know that schools that serve  dominantly white students uh receive 23  billion dollars a year in additional  funds than schools that don't  that's a problem that's a conversation  to have so they're not living up fully  to the um  possibility of providing an equal  opportunity um but going in the exact  opposite direction  um certainly won't get us any closer uh  to public schools realizing that  potential  okay okay let me see what else do i have  here  okay  um  do you are you aware of how curriculum  is decided for schools  yeah so generally speaking um that is a  decision um by either a  statewide governing agency  a board of regents or  state superintendent  different states have different uh  phrasings and words for that  local school districts  have some autonomy in that that decision  and that process as well  and then down to the local teacher um  the teachers are prescribed a a very  specific set of standards  uh that must be taught um it does i can  say as a former history teacher it does  generally feel  overwhelming to try to teach so many  standards in so little time  um especially with with the increase in  and preparing students for uh  standardized tests  that may or may not even be connected to  that content  um but as a local teacher you have some  uh leeway and and  not necessarily what's top but how it's  taught right we understand that teachers  are the experts and and the  professionals in the classroom  but there's a system in place from the  teacher to the local school board to the  state apparatus  that makes those decisions and the  benefit of public schools  is that at every level  there's public input right whether that  is  in deciding who is the state  superintendent through an election  um or  even if that's an appointed position say  by governor uh  people having say so and who that  governor might be  to the local district and even to the  local school with with the principal and  the teachers at every point there's an  opportunity for public input in public  oversight as it should be  whereas  other types of school delivery whether  it be private schools or or the epitome  of it in homeschooling  there's little to no public oversight  into what curriculum is taught how it's  selected and how it's delivered  uh you mentioned private schools there  for a second so is there  a major difference between public  schools and private schools in regards  to what they're being taught  yeah and and just speaking on that note  um  public schools um have full autonomy in  their curriculum uh in their admissions  uh process uh a a publix or excuse me a  private school that is religious in  nature uh can have a litmus test on a  prospective student uh fitting within a  very specific  understanding of a religious  denomination um or  to whether they're accepted or not  certainly that's not allowed in the  public school space  but to the curriculum space as well  private schools represent an autonomous  body that make decisions about student  entry student discipline hiring firing  and curriculum all on their own with  zero  outside or public oversight  interesting okay  okay well i think that covers  what i wanted to ask you about  homeschooling is there anything else you  wanted to mention while you're here  yeah thanks for that opportunity again i  would i would conclude as it were um  reiterating this this question or this  notion of  how do we as a society conceptualize  education and the importance of it uh  and if it is something that we  conceptualize and understand to be of  vital importance  for children for their development and  their lived realities both as a child  but also in preparation for adulthood  is that something that warrants  a space  where where they go uh to engage in that  process with trained professionals  or is that something that that anybody  can do and in the case of homeschooling  that their parents can do  um  is having a high school diploma  good as it is is that sufficient  for teaching children um not just  kindergarten but all the way through  advanced trigonometry as a high schooler  those are some important questions to  ask  again as a society  we we generally would not condone  parents choosing or electing  to provide medical care for their  children  or firefighting care for their house  right sort of pulling away asking for  their tax money back from the local fire  department um those are not things that  as a society we would really  um  allow or or be okay with uh and so when  it comes to education the fact that we  are readily okay with that for children  should raise some concern um but again  it gives us the opportunity as a society  to think through uh what does it mean to  educate a child uh and if that is an  important process should we and at least  in my view we should um  rely on trained educated professionals  to do that with the opportunity for  parents to be engaged in that process uh  whether that's through the political  process of  speaking at local school boards or  running for a local school board or just  in conversation with their teachers  it does take a village to raise a child  we need to understand that  and it seems to me that public schools  represent the better opportunity to  engage in that process  uh than than a child being kept at home  okay  thank you very much for coming on if if  people want to find you online where can  they go  yeah i have a website it's t  jamesonbrewer.com um and my twitter  handle is the same it's at t jamison  brewer and uh through there you can  connect with me in myriad ways  okay thank you very much  let me say michaela 